The coexistence of multiple generations within contemporary workplaces has transformed intergenerational conflict from a peripheral management issue into a structural feature of the future of work. Differences in values, expectations regarding hierarchy, flexibility, performance metrics, and perceptions of authority increasingly shape organizational dynamics and directly affect labor relations. This article argues that such conflicts should be understood not merely as cultural or behavioral challenges, but as a significant source of labor risk with direct implications for legal compliance, corporate governance, and organizational sustainability.&nbsp;
<div>
 By examining the interaction between intergenerational tensions and emerging work arrangements—such as remote work, platform-based labor, algorithmic management, and flexible working time—the paper demonstrates how traditional models of labor compliance, focused primarily on formal adherence to statutory rules, are insufficient to address these evolving risks. Intergenerational misalignment often manifests in legally relevant practices, including structural moral harassment, indirect age discrimination, abusive performance management, excessive monitoring, and the neglect of psychosocial health obligations, frequently without explicit intent to violate labor law.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 The article proposes a reconceptualization of labor compliance as a preventive governance strategy, emphasizing the integration of intergenerational sensitivity into risk assessment, internal policies, leadership practices, and monitoring mechanisms. It further situates labor compliance within broader corporate governance and ESG frameworks, highlighting the centrality of the “social” dimension in mitigating reputational, legal, and economic exposure. Ultimately, the paper contends that managing intergenerational conflict is emerging as a new frontier of labor compliance in the evolving landscape of work.&nbsp;
</div>

Contemporary economic, legal, and organizational institutions remain predominantly grounded in a cognitive paradigm that may be described as predictive rationality. Within this paradigm, rationality is understood as the capacity of agents and designers to anticipate future states of the world with sufficient accuracy to justify optimal ex‑ante planning. Contracts, policies, and governance structures are therefore treated as instruments for locking in expected futures through specification, optimization, and control.&nbsp;
<div>
 While predictive rationality has historically enabled large‑scale coordination in relatively stable environments, its epistemic limitations have become increasingly salient in contemporary socio‑economic systems. Digitalization, global interconnection, and rapid technological recombination have transformed many institutional environments into complex adaptive systems characterized by non‑linearity, endogenous preference formation, and emergent dynamics. In such contexts, the future cannot be reliably inferred from the past, and institutional rigidity becomes a source of fragility rather than stability.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 This paper develops Demand‑Driven Contractual Closed‑Loop Theory (DDCCLT) as a philosophical and institutional response to these conditions. It introduces feedback rationality as a complementary mode of institutional cognition focused on continuous correction, learning, and adaptation during execution rather than exhaustive prediction at the design stage. Drawing on American Pragmatism, Cybernetics, Process Philosophy, and contemporary complexity economics, the paper reconstructs the epistemic foundations of contractual governance.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 The central thesis is that DDCCLT does not replace predictive rationality but functions as a second‑order corrective mechanism operating at the execution layer of institutions. By embedding structured feedback loops that translate demand signals into contractual adjustment, DDCCLT addresses the structural incompleteness of static contracts and enhances institutional robustness under uncertainty
</div>
